    wellnessDomain: 'Intellectual',
    theme: 'Ownership',
    point: 'Six: Recovery Disciplines',
    leadIn: [],
    commentary: [
      'We visit this theme again because it bears revisiting. At the Empower phase of recovery, blame is a particular kind of trap. Early in recovery, understanding the roots of addiction — trauma, family patterns, social circumstances — is important. But the Empower phase asks something different: having understood all of that, what do you choose now?',
      'The circumstances that shaped your addiction are real. The harm done to you was real. The systemic injustices that increased your risk were real. None of that is denied. But recovery is not possible while waiting for the world to become fair. It is not possible while waiting for the people who hurt you to apologize. It is not possible while holding circumstances responsible for your choices. Recovery is the act of claiming authority over your next action, regardless of what came before.',
      "This is not the same as saying \"your struggles are your fault.\" Blame and responsibility are different things. Blame looks backward and assigns fault. Responsibility looks forward and claims agency. Blame keeps you stuck in the past. Responsibility moves you into action in the present. At GFA, we honor the past — we don't pretend it didn't shape you. And we insist on agency in the present — we don't pretend you're powerless.",
    ],
    practice:
      'Identify one area of your current recovery where you\'ve been waiting for something external to change before you take action. Name it specifically. Then ask: "What action can I take right now, regardless of whether that external thing changes?" Take that action this week. This is what ownership feels like.',
