      "When things go well — stay steady. Don't let good news become complacency. Don't mistake a good day for a reason to ease up on your practices. Don't let achievement become pride that distances you from the community. When things go badly — stay steady. Don't let hard times become catastrophe. Don't let a difficult day signal the end of everything. Don't abandon your practices when they're needed most.",
      'The Anointed One demonstrated this equanimity consistently. Welcomed with palm branches and condemned in the same week. His response to both was the same quality of presence. Paul echoes this: "I have learned, in whatever state I am, to be content." Learned — not received, not given. It is a practice.',
